---
// New page, AEO sprint 2026-10-28. Answers the question the Lake Powell depth page raises in
// one FAQ line ("why do other sites show a different elevation than this page?") as a general
// explanation a reader can apply to any lake on the site.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';
import { breadcrumbLd, absUrl, ORG_ID } from '../../lib/seo';
import { getReading, fmtNum, POWELL_BAND } from '../../lib/lakes';

const PATH = '/guides/why-lake-elevations-differ/';
const title = 'Why two sites show a different lake elevation on the same day';
const description =
  'Three things make lake elevations disagree between sites: the vertical datum, the gage the number comes from, and the hour it was read. How to tell which difference you are looking at.';
const UPDATED = 'October 28, 2026';

const powell = getReading('lake-powell');
const elev = powell?.level_ft ?? null;

const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `${absUrl(PATH)}#article`,
  headline: title,
  description,
  url: absUrl(PATH),
  mainEntityOfPage: absUrl(PATH),
  datePublished: '2026-10-28',
  dateModified: '2026-10-28',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Michael', url: absUrl('/about/') },
  publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
};
const ld = [articleLd, breadcrumbLd([
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Why lake elevations differ', path: PATH },
])];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={PATH} jsonLd={ld}>
  <div class="container">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Why two sites show a different lake elevation</h1>
      <p>
        Two pages can quote the same reservoir on the same morning and disagree by a foot without
        either of them being wrong. There are three reasons, and they are easy to separate once you
        know what to look for.
      </p>

      <h2 id="datum">1. A different vertical datum</h2>
      <p>
        An elevation is a height above a reference surface, and the United States has more than one.
        The current national vertical datum is NAVD 88; older records and older engineering drawings
        often use NGVD 29. The gap between them is not one number: it varies with location, so the two
        datums do not convert by adding a constant. The National Geodetic Survey documents both at
        <a href="https://www.ngs.noaa.gov/datums/vertical/" rel="nofollow noopener">ngs.noaa.gov/datums/vertical</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A site that mixes datums across lakes produces a table where the numbers look comparable and are
        not. This site prints the agency figure for each lake without shifting it, and where a page
        derives a depth it subtracts two elevations that are on the same datum.
      </p>

      <h2 id="gage">2. A different gage</h2>
      <p>
        A reservoir is not a flat sheet. Wind, inflow and release points move the surface, and the gage
        that produces the number sits at one place. For Lake Powell the reading comes from USGS site
        09379900 at Glen Canyon Dam, parameter 62614, the reservoir water-surface elevation series. A
        site quoting a different site number is quoting a different point on the water.
      </p>
      <p>
        Storage-based figures add a second step. Percent full is storage divided by capacity, and
        storage comes from a rating table that converts elevation to volume. Two sites can agree on the
        elevation to the hundredth of a foot and still disagree on percent full.
      </p>

      <h2 id="time">3. A different hour</h2>
      <p>
        USGS instantaneous gages update every 15 to 60 minutes. Daily reservoir storage summaries are
        published once a day. A page built at 09:00 and a page built at 15:00 can legitimately differ,
        and a page that caches its build for a week can be six days behind while looking live.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every reading on this site carries its own timestamp, and the lake pages print it next to the
        gauge. Check the timestamp before you check the number.
      </p>

      <h2 id="apply">Applying it to a lake here</h2>
      <p>
        {elev == null
          ? 'Lake Powell has no current reading in this build, so no comparison can be shown right now.'
          : `Lake Powell reads ${fmtNum(elev, 2)} ft today. Full pool is ${fmtNum(POWELL_BAND.fullPoolFt, 0)} ft and dead pool is ${fmtNum(POWELL_BAND.deadPoolFt, 0)} ft, so the surface is ${fmtNum(elev - POWELL_BAND.deadPoolFt, 1)} ft above the dam's hard floor.`}
        The <a href="/lake/lake-powell/depth/">Lake Powell depth page</a> shows the same elevation with
        the depth it implies at the dam, and the
        <a href="/guides/dead-pool-and-minimum-power-pool/">dead pool guide</a> explains the two
        operating floors the number is measured against.
      </p>
      <p class="mut" style="font-size:12px;">Updated {UPDATED}.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
